        "title": "The historical context of attitudes to work",
        "text": "For much of human history, work has been hard and degrading. But while it has always been accepted that work is necessary to satisfy material needs, attitudes towards working hard when there is no compulsion to do so have varied over the centuries.\n\nNearly 3,000 years ago, the ancient Greeks regarded work as a curse. Philosophers such as Plato and Aristotle made it clear that most men laboured so that a minority could devote themselves to pure exercises of the mind: art, philosophy and politics. According to Plato, \"Those who need to work must be willing to accept an inferior status.\"\n\nDuring the Middle Ages, from about 400 AD until 1400 AD, work was still seen negatively, though people had a positive attitude towards earnings that kept them from depending on the charity of others for the physical needs of life. Wealth was seen as an opportunity to share with those who might be less fortunate, and so work that produced wealth became acceptable. However, any attempt to build up excessive wealth was frowned upon. It was a worker's duty to stay in his class, passing his family's work on from father to son.\n\nThe Reformation, a period of religious and political upheaval in Western Europe during the sixteenth century, brought a new view of work. Two key religious leaders who influenced the development of Western culture at this time were Martin Luther, from Germany, and John Calvin, from France.\n\nBuilding on Luther's ideas, John Calvin introduced an important new attitude to work. He taught that people's daily life and actions, and their success in worldly activities, reflected their moral worth. Calvin believed that all men must work, even the rich. Men were not to wish for wealth, possessions or an easy life, but were to put the profits of their work back into financing further ventures. Choosing an occupation and pursuing it to achieve the greatest possible profit was considered a religious duty, even if that meant leaving the family trade or profession. The key elements of these new beliefs about work, usually called the Protestant work ethic, were hard work, punctuality, working for long-term (rather than immediate) benefits, and the great importance of work.\n\nIn time, these attitudes became the norm in Western culture. As the industrial revolution gathered pace in the nineteenth century, the idea of work as a religious duty was replaced by the idea of public usefulness. Economists warned of the poverty and decline that would strike the country if people failed to work hard, and moralists stressed every person's social duty to be productive.\n\nNow let us move forward to the present, to the information age, which began in the 1980s. To a far greater extent than before, work is now seen as rewarding in itself. This work ethic stresses skill, challenge, independence, recognition and the quality of the work produced. Independence, or autonomy, has been identified as a particularly important factor in job satisfaction. The motivation to work involves trust, caring, meaning, self-knowledge, challenge, opportunities for personal growth, and dignity, rather than purely financial incentives. Workers want control over their work, something that was lost with the mechanisation of the industrial age, and many modern jobs make this possible. As a result, the work ethic has gained a personal meaning that was missing from most jobs in the industrial age. Some studies have found that fewer people now believe that hard work will bring adequate rewards. This is a significant shift, because pay and \"getting ahead\" were the main incentives management used to encourage productivity during the industrial age.\n\nTo sum up, today's work ethic places a positive moral value on doing a good job, and is based on the belief that work is valuable for its own sake."
